{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"securing-s3-buckets","skill_description":"Create and secure S3 buckets following AWS best practices for access control, encryption, monitoring, and remediation of misconfigurations. Use when the user wants to secure a new bucket, audit an existing bucket, fix a security finding, configure encryption, or enable logging and monitoring. Do NOT use for general S3 data operations, S3 Tables setup, or discovering existing data assets.\n\n\nServices: s3, kms, cloudtrail, guardduty, config, accessanalyzer\nTasks: secure, audit, remediate, encrypt, monitor\nPersona: developer, security-engineer\nWorkload: storage, compliance\n*","skill_name":"securing-s3-buckets"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon S3 Metadata is now available in three additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 Metadata, a feature that enables users to discover and understand their S3 data with automated and easily-queried metadata, is now available in three additional AWS Regions: Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), and Asia Pacific (Tokyo). This expansion makes S3 Metadata generally available in six AWS Regions. S3 Metadata supports both system-defined metadata, such as size and source of the object, and custom metadata, which includes tags for annotating objects with additional information. The metadata is automatically populated for both new and existing objects, providing a comprehensive, queryable view of data. S3 Metadata helps with business analytics, real-time inference applications, and more. For pricing details, visit the S3 pricing page. To learn more, check out the product page, documentation, and AWS Storage Blog","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-s3-metadata-three-additional-regions/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS Textract documentation on S3 access permission model for sync vs async APIs."}
